<TopicPage slug="subjects/law/intermediate/how-a-case-moves-through-court">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Law lesson. The foundational lesson What makes a law showed
    where legal rules come from, and the sibling lesson Criminal versus civil law
    established the two tracks and their standards of proof. Here you learn the
    machinery those lessons left out: the stages, deadlines and personnel that decide
    when a case actually ends — usually long before any courtroom scene.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the three threshold questions — jurisdiction, limitation, standing — that can end a case before its merits are heard;</li>
    <li>trace the civil route from pre-action protocol to trial, and say where most cases settle and why;</li>
    <li>trace the criminal route from charge to verdict, including how venue is chosen and what a guilty plea buys;</li>
    <li>say what each appellate rung reviews, and why appeal courts do not re-hear witnesses;</li>
    <li>identify the division of labour among solicitors, barristers, judges, magistrates and juries.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A lawsuit is <strong>a structured argument with checkpoints</strong> — each stage a filter
    designed to resolve cases that shouldn't need trial. Most people imagine the courtroom; in
    reality the overwhelming majority of civil matters settle during evidence exchange, and
    appeals rarely revisit facts. The procedural map is not boring plumbing: it is the answer
    to the practical question everyone actually has — <em>what will happen, and when will this
    end?</em> Learn the filters and you can predict where a case will die, settle or survive.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five stages, from filing to precedent" widget="equationStep" config={caseCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each stage is a filter with an exit — pleading can
    dismiss, discovery can settle, summary judgment can end a case without any trial,
    and appeal reviews law rather than facts. Step through the panels and watch the
    population of surviving cases shrink at every gate; the courtroom scene of stage 4
    is what remains after four earlier filters have done their work.
  </p>

  <h2>Before the merits: jurisdiction, limitation, standing</h2>
  <p>
    Three threshold questions can end a case before anyone hears its substance.
    <strong>Jurisdiction</strong>: does this court have power over this subject matter, this
    territory, and this defendant? Sue in the wrong court and the case is dismissed however
    meritorious. <strong>Limitation</strong>: claims expire. The Limitation Act 1980 gives
    most contract and tort claims six years, personal-injury claims three, defamation one —
    and deadlines are close to absolute: a valid claim issued a month late is usually dead,
    which is why firms have diaries full of limitation dates. <strong>Standing</strong>: the
    claimant must be the right claimant — the person whose right is at stake. Public law has
    the same gates in <strong>judicial review</strong>, which challenges the legality of
    government decisions, with its own brutal clock ("promptly, and in any event within three
    months"). Procedural rules look technical precisely because they are the law's way of
    refusing to hear stale, misdirected or meddlesome claims — and every rule is also a
    trap that has killed real cases.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each gate with what it demands" widget="match" config={cmGatesMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a strong claim survives a missed deadline." The
  limitation deadlines are close to absolute: a valid personal-injury claim issued a
  month late is usually dead whatever its merits, which is why law firms keep diaries
  full of limitation dates. Merit decides whether a case should win; procedure
  decides whether it is heard at all.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three threshold questions can kill a claim before anyone
    argues its substance. A claim that survives them enters the managed pipeline of
    the civil route — where the biggest filter of all is waiting.
  </p>

  <h2>The civil route: claim to trial</h2>
  <p>
    Civil litigation in England and Wales runs on the Civil Procedure Rules (CPR), the
    product of Lord Woolf's <em>Access to Justice</em> review (1996, in force 1999), whose core
    idea was to replace lawyer-controlled combat with <strong>judicial case management</strong>.
    The sequence: pre-action protocols require parties to exchange letters and documents
    before suing; the claimant issues a <strong>claim form</strong> and
    <strong>particulars of claim</strong>; the defendant has 14 days (extendable) to file a
    <strong>defence</strong> and any counterclaim. The court then allocates the case to a
    <strong>track</strong> by value and complexity — small claims (roughly under 10,000 pounds,
    heard informally, no costs-shifting), fast track, or multi-track — and a judge actively
    sets directions: dates for <strong>disclosure</strong> of documents, exchange of witness
    statements, expert reports, then trial. Disclosure is usually the longest and most
    decisive phase: once each side sees the other's evidence, the fog of dispute clears and
    settlement follows — which is the system working, since a trial is the most expensive way
    to resolve anything. At trial, a judge (sitting alone in almost all civil cases) hears
    evidence, finds facts on the balance of probabilities, applies the law, and — because
    <strong>costs usually follow the event</strong> — orders the loser to pay most of the
    winner's costs, the structural pressure that shapes every tactical decision upstream.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="line up the civil pipeline as Woolf designed it" widget="order" config={cmCivilOrder} />
  <p>
    The most elegant settlement engine is the formal offer procedure ( CPR Part 36 ): either
    side can put a sealed offer on the table with built-in cost consequences — refuse a
    claimant's offer and then fail to beat it at trial, and the claimant collects extra
    costs and enhanced interest; beat your own defendant's offer, and the defendant pays
    extra. The rule weaponises information and self-assessment: each side must privately
    price its case, and guessing wrong is expensive. It turns the abstract encouragement to
    settle into arithmetic, and a large share of cases close the day after an offer expires.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="play out the Part 36 offer" widget="branch" config={cmOfferBranch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the courtroom scene is the endpoint. In fact the trial is
    the exception — a small fraction of issued claims — and even cases that reach trial often
    settle in the corridor on day one, once witnesses have been heard and the odds are priced.
  </p>

  <h2>The criminal route: charge to verdict</h2>
  <p>
    Criminal procedure starts with investigation, then a <strong>charge</strong> (by the CPS
    in England and Wales, applying a two-part test: is there sufficient evidence, and is a
    prosecution in the public interest?). The venue decision follows the offence class:
    <strong>summary offences</strong> stay in the magistrates' court (roughly 95 per cent of
    cases); <strong>indictable-only offences</strong> (murder, rape, robbery) go straight to
    the Crown Court; <strong>either-way offences</strong> (theft, many assaults) give the
    defendant a choice of mode of trial — magistrates (faster, sentencing capped at 12
    months) or Crown Court (jury, unlimited sentence). At the first hearing the defendant
    <strong>pleads</strong>; a guilty plea short-circuits everything (earning a sentencing
    discount of up to a third, the system's main lubricant), and a not-guilty plea sets a
    trial. Before trial, the prosecution must <strong>disclose</strong> its evidence and any
    material that might undermine its case — the safeguard built after notorious miscarriages
    (the Guildford Four and Birmingham Six cases, whose collapse led to the Criminal Appeal
    Act 1995 and the Crown Prosecution Service's independence). At the Crown Court, twelve
    jurors decide the verdict on the judge's directions of law; conviction requires
    unanimity or a 10–2 majority; then the judge passes <strong>sentence</strong> under the
    guidelines of the Sentencing Council, weighing aggravating and mitigating factors.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file each offence class by its venue" widget="sort-buckets" config={cmVenueBuckets} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> both routes are built to end cases early — disclosure
    settles civil claims, guilty pleas short-circuit criminal ones. When a case does
    run its full course, the question becomes not who wins but who is allowed to look
    at the result again.
  </p>

  <h2>Appeals: the ladder and what each rung reviews</h2>
  <p>
    The appellate structure is a filter pyramid. From the magistrates' court, a defendant can
    appeal to the Crown Court (a full rehearing) or, on a point of law, by "case stated" to
    the High Court. From the Crown Court, appeal lies to the <strong>Court of Appeal
    (Criminal Division)</strong> — but only with <strong>leave</strong> (permission), granted
    where the conviction is "unsafe"; the Criminal Appeal Act 1995 widened this to include
    fresh evidence, and the Criminal Cases Review Commission (created 1997) exists
    specifically to send possibly-wrong convictions back. From the civil courts the route
    goes High Court → Court of Appeal (Civil Division) → <strong>Supreme Court</strong>
    (created by the Constitutional Reform Act 2005, sitting since 2009, replacing the Law
    Lords). At every rung the same principle applies: appeal courts correct <em>errors of
    law and procedure</em> and check that verdicts are "safe" — they do not re-hear the
    witnesses. Deference to trial findings is not laziness; the trial judge <em>saw the
    witnesses</em>, the appellate court reads a transcript. The final rung converts case into
    precedent: a Supreme Court ruling becomes the binding rule the next thousand cases
    inherit, which is why leave to appeal is granted in only a small minority of
    applications — the system rations its precedent-making capacity.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — what each rung reviews" widget="judge" config={cmAppealJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "an appeal is a second trial with better lawyers."
  Appellate courts correct errors of law and procedure and check that verdicts are
  "safe"; they do not re-hear witnesses, because the trial court saw them and the
  appeal court reads only a transcript. That deference is the design, not laziness —
  and it is one reason leave to appeal is granted in only a small minority of
  applications.</p>

  <h2>The legal personnel: who actually runs the system</h2>
  <p>
    The map is staffed by a distinctive division of labour. <strong>Solicitors</strong> advise
    clients, draft documents and run cases day to day; <strong>barristers</strong> are
    specialist advocates instructed for court — the traditional split (both can now appear in
    higher courts, but the specialist bar still dominates the Crown Court and above).
    <strong>Judges</strong> are appointed (since 2006) through the independent Judicial
    Appointments Commission — a reform meant to end the old "secret garden" of tap-on-the-
    shoulder patronage — and their independence is the system's load-bearing wall: paid from
    the Consolidated Fund, tenure protected, removal only by address in Parliament. The
    senior judiciary's role changed character in the twentieth century, from "neutral
    umpires who only interpret" to a court (the Supreme Court) that can and does push back on
    executive power — the <em>Miller</em> judgments being the modern proof. Laypeople remain
    embedded throughout: magistrates (volunteers) decide the bulk of criminal cases, juries
    decide the serious ones — an insistence that justice is done by the community, not only
    by professionals.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each role with its job" widget="match" config={cmPersonnelMatch} />

  <h2>Funding: access to justice and who pays</h2>
  <p>
    Procedure is worthless if you cannot afford to use it, which makes funding the least
    glamorous and most consequential topic in the unit. <strong>Criminal legal aid</strong>
    survives (the state pays for defence representation, means-tested), on the theory that
    the state cannot prosecute someone it has also deprived of counsel.
    <strong>Civil legal aid</strong> was drastically cut by the LASPO Act 2012 — removing it
    for most family, housing, welfare-benefit and immigration disputes — and the vacuum was
    partly filled by <strong>conditional fee agreements</strong> ("no win, no fee") and
    after-the-event insurance, which work for strong money claims but not for the
    unglamorous cases (benefits appeals, eviction defences) where need is greatest. The
    measured result: a collapse in legal-aid-funded civil cases and a surge in litigants in
    person — people arguing their own cases before judges built for a two-sided contest.
    Funding decisions therefore decide the system's real caseload, whatever the rules say
    on paper.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="drag the divider — the funded track and the cut one" widget="compare" config={cmFundingCompare} />

  <h2>Anatomy of a trial: what actually happens on the day</h2>
  <p>
    The ceremony follows a fixed script designed for fairness under pressure of time. In a
    civil trial: counsel open with short speeches stating what they intend to prove;
    <strong>claimant's evidence first</strong> — witnesses give their statements-in-chief
    (now often read, since written statements are exchanged in advance) and are
    <strong>cross-examined</strong> by the other side, then re-examined; each witness swears
    an oath or affirms; documents are proved through witnesses. Then the defendant's case,
    same order. <strong>Closing speeches</strong>, and — in a jury trial — the judge's
    <strong>summing-up</strong>: the law, and the rival versions of the facts, set out
    neutrally. In the criminal Crown Court the same skeleton applies, with the prosecutor
    opening first and carrying the burden throughout; the defendant has the right not to
    give evidence, though adverse inferences may now be drawn (Criminal Justice and Public
    Order Act 1994, a genuine controversy between fairness and the fight against silent
    defendants). The style is more managerial than the American television version: English
    judges control questioning, exclude irrelevant material, and steer pace — advocacy here
    is a chess game of precise questions, not monologues.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="stage the trial day in order" widget="order" config={cmTrialOrder} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>At which stage of a civil claim does the largest fraction settle, and why?</li>
    <li>A personal-injury claim is issued three years and one month after the accident. What happens?</li>
    <li>Who decides the verdict in a Crown Court trial, and what does the judge do?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) During disclosure and evidence exchange — once each
    side sees the strength of the other's hand, both can price the case realistically,
    and asymmetric information (the main fuel of disputes) is removed. (2) It is
    likely statute-barred: the Limitation Act 1980 sets a three-year deadline, and
    missing it is usually fatal regardless of merit. (3) The jury of twelve decides
    the verdict on the judge's directions of law — facts to the jury, law to the
    judge; in civil trials the judge usually does both jobs alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A lawsuit is a structured argument with checkpoints, and most cases end at one of them.</li>
    <li>Jurisdiction, limitation and standing can end a case before its merits are ever heard.</li>
    <li>Disclosure is usually the longest and most decisive civil phase — settlement is the system's normal output.</li>
    <li>Formal offers (CPR Part 36) turn the encouragement to settle into arithmetic.</li>
    <li>Criminal venue follows the offence class; a guilty plea earns a discount and short-circuits trial.</li>
    <li>Appeal courts review law, procedure and safety — never the witnesses — and the top rung makes precedent.</li>
    <li>Funding decides the real caseload: LASPO moved civil justice toward no-win-no-fee and litigants in person.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling lesson Sources of law completes the picture by showing what the appeal
    courts' precedents actually feed back into, and the advanced lessons Torts and
    liability and Contracts put flesh on the claims this procedure carries. Watch one
    real claim form's journey and the map will hold.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer is proportionality and strain. The Woolf reforms aimed to make procedure
    proportionate — "just, expeditious and economical" — and case management genuinely
    shortened ordinary cases; but the deeper pressures remain. The crown court backlog
    exceeded 60,000 cases in the mid-2020s, the highest on record, meaning years between
    charge and trial — a delay that damages witnesses, evidence and the presumption of
    innocence alike. Digital reform (online claims, virtual hearings accelerated by the
    pandemic) is reshaping the map in real time. And the boundary of the system is
    <strong>alternative dispute resolution</strong>: mediation, arbitration and ombudsmen
    now resolve volumes the courts once did, raising the genuine open question of what
    courts are <em>for</em> — the answer the system has settled on is public, enforceable
    justice and precedent: the private mechanisms borrow the courts' shadow, but only
    courts make the rules the shadow is cast by. One final reframing to carry forward:
    every filter in this map — permission stages, leave to appeal, limitation deadlines —
    is a deliberate trade of individual cases for system capacity, and arguing about any
    of them is really arguing about where that trade should sit.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> standard civil procedure texts; Glanville Williams, Learning the Law; Zuckerman on Civil Procedure; Woolf, Access to Justice (1996); Criminal Appeal Act 1995; Constitutional Reform Act 2005. Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
